# Harness

> **Name pending.** Working title is `Harness`. Candidates: **Apex Lattice** (layered-composition), **Apexon Strata** (compliance/overlay), **ApexForge** (craft-a-harness), **Apex Cadence** (phase-rhythm).

> **Multi-target support coming.** v1 emits `.claude/` for Claude Code today. The harness IR is tool-agnostic; emitters for `.codex/`, `.github/`, `.cursor/`, `.gemini/` are stubbed and land in v2.

Configuration-driven SDLC harness generator. Reads a single `harness.yaml`, emits a working `.claude/` tree with skills, agents, hooks, rules, and a state contract.

Harness's job is **governance and discipline**, not workflow plumbing. The harness encodes a phased SDLC (intake → research → plan → implement → validate → review → security-review) where every phase writes a verifiable state contract, every artefact has a traceability id, every agent honors path-scoped rules.

---

## Platform requirements

Harness's emitted skills shell out to standard Unix utilities (`find`, `stat`, `file`, `pdftotext`, etc.) via Claude Code's Bash tool. These are available on:

- **macOS** — natively
- **Linux** — natively
- **Windows** — requires [WSL](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/) (Windows Subsystem for Linux) or Git Bash; native PowerShell/CMD is not supported

---

## Quick start

```bash
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install git+https://github.com/Apexon-Enterprise-org/agent-rise-foundry.git

mkdir my-app && cd my-app
harness init               # silent default: minimal harness.yaml, no prompts
harness generate           # render .claude/ + .mcp.json + CLAUDE.md
# open the project in Claude Code; /arh-intake "<feature>" works immediately
```

Three commands to first story. **Zero hard choices at init.** Stacks, integrations, and governance are deferred — declare them via `harness add` when you actually need them.

Pass `--interactive` if you prefer the 10-prompt wizard:

```bash
harness init --interactive
```

### Add stacks / integrations / governance when ready

```bash
harness add stack nextjs:15 --pm pnpm --test-runner vitest --paths "apps/web/**"
harness add stack fastapi:0.115 --pm uv --test-runner pytest --paths "services/api/**"

harness add integration issue-tracker jira          # MCP discovery runs at /arh-init
harness add integration doc-tracker confluence
harness add integration design claude-design        # claude.ai/design handoff seam

harness add governance hipaa                        # compliance overlay scaffolds

harness add domain healthcare                       # domain-aware /arh-generate-prd + research
harness add domain pharmaceutical                   # custom domain → 5-step wizard
harness add domain list                             # see all 9 bundled domain profiles
```

> **Migration note:** Domain detection is now **opt-in**. Projects upgrading from earlier versions should run `harness add domain <name>` to restore detection. Projects with no domain configured emit an empty `domains.json` and get a clean generic PRD — zero drift.

Each command edits `harness.yaml`, re-runs `harness generate`, and prints the before→after. Pass `--no-regenerate` to batch edits and run `harness generate` once at the end.

> **Declare BEFORE `/arh-init`.** Run all the `harness add` commands you need before invoking `/arh-init` in Claude Code. `/arh-init` reads your declared providers and walks **MCP discovery** to populate `docs/config/*.yaml` with real values (cloudId, project_key, issue-type ids, space_key, etc.). Skipping the `harness add ...` step means `/arh-init` defaults everything to `none` / `local` and you re-run.

**Why deferred:** `/arh-intake`, `/arh-validate-story`, `/arh-research`, `/plan-requirements` work without a stack — stories describe *what* users do, not *how* code does it. Stack is needed at the `/plan-implementation` boundary.

In a Claude Code session, the `.claude/` tree wires the slash commands listed in [Slash commands](#slash-commands) below.

For a full copy-paste walkthrough from empty directory to first PR, see [documents/first-30-minutes.md](documents/first-30-minutes.md).

---

## CLI commands

Commands you run in your shell (not inside Claude Code). The `harness` CLI manages the harness itself — config, generation, validation, packaging — separate from the in-session slash commands [below](#slash-commands). Detailed flags and options live in [documents/commands.md](documents/commands.md).

| # | Command | What it is | How to use | Reason for using |
|---|---------|------------|------------|------------------|
| 1 | `harness init` | Writes `harness.yaml`. Silent by default (empty stacks + safe defaults). | `harness init` (silent default) or `harness init --interactive` for the 10-prompt wizard.<br>Flags: `--config / -c <path>` (default `harness.yaml`), `--force` (overwrite existing), `--interactive / -i`, `--target / -t <claude-code \| codex \| copilot \| gemini \| cursor>`. | First step in any new project. Defers stack / integration / governance choices to `harness add` so init is zero-friction. |
| 2 | `harness generate` | Renders the harness from `harness.yaml` — emits `.claude/`, `.mcp.json`, `CLAUDE.md`, and the state schema. | `harness generate`.<br>Flags: `--config / -c <path>`, `--out / -o <dir>` (default `.`), `--target / -t <emitter>`, `--dry-run` (preview only), `--diff` (unified diff vs existing files), `--legacy-commands` (also emit `.claude/commands/<n>.md` for AD-mobile parity). | The core build step. Re-run after any `harness add` or any edit to `harness.yaml`. |
| 3 | `harness validate` | Schema-checks `harness.yaml` and lints the emitted `.claude/` tree against F-001..F-051 (43 rules). | `harness validate`.<br>Flags: `--config / -c <path>`, `--out / -o <dir>` (where `.claude/` lives), `--strict` (warnings → errors). | Catches malformed config and emitted-output regressions. Wire into CI; `--strict` upgrades warnings (e.g. unfilled TODO slots) to errors. |
| 4 | `harness analyse` | Reads a Claude Code session JSONL and emits an HTML timeline — phases, interventions, outcomes. | `harness analyse --latest` for the most recent session, `harness analyse --session <id>`, or `harness analyse --all` to stitch every session.<br>Flags: `--project / -p <slug>`, `--out / -o <dir>` (default `harness-reports`), `--format / -f <html \| json \| md \| all>`, `--watch / -w` (live dashboard), `--watch-interval <sec>` (default 5), `--watch-synth` (run LLM synthesis each tick), `--no-llm` (skip narrative synthesis), `--model / -m <name>` (default `sonnet`), `--list-projects`, `--list-sessions`. | Post-session retro: where did the session stall, which intervention unblocked it, what's the phase distribution. Feeds into `harness tune`. |
| 5 | `harness add` | Appends a stack, integration, governance profile, or domain to `harness.yaml` and re-runs `harness generate`. | Subcommands:<br>• `harness add stack <fw>[:<ver>]` — flags: `--stack-id <id>`, `--package-manager / --pm <pm>`, `--test-runner <runner>`, `--paths "<globs>"`<br>• `harness add integration <type> <provider>` — type ∈ `issue-tracker \| doc-tracker \| design`<br>• `harness add governance <profile>` — profile ∈ `standard \| strict \| hipaa \| pci \| sox \| gdpr`<br>• `harness add domain <name>` — name ∈ `healthcare \| fintech \| defense \| retail \| edtech \| saas \| insurance \| legaltech \| govtech \| <custom>`. Use `list` to see all. Custom domains trigger a 5-step suggest-and-confirm wizard.<br>Common flags: `--config / -c <path>`, `--no-regenerate` (skip the implicit `harness generate`). | The incremental composition surface. Adds one overlay at a time and prints the before→after, so you see exactly what changed. |
| 6 | `harness site` | Generates a MkDocs site from the project's `docs/`, `.claude/`, and `CLAUDE.md` — feature dashboards, status board, RTM, decisions / clarifications / agent flags. | `harness site` (writes to `harness-site/`) or `harness site --serve` to build + serve at `http://127.0.0.1:8000`.<br>Flags: `--root / -r <dir>`, `--out / -o <dir>` (default `harness-site`), `--serve`, `--port <n>` (default 8000), `--no-build` (markdown only, skip mkdocs build), `--clean` (remove `--out` first), `--include-internal` (surface `.claude/rules/` under Reference), `--strict` (fail on mkdocs warnings), `--logo <path>`, `--favicon <path>`, `--kpi-window <days>` (default 7). | Non-technical readers (PM, exec, design, audit) need a browsable view of the project. Replaces "grep through markdown" with an indexed site. |

### Helpful commands

> _Diagnostic / power-user commands._ Useful but not part of the regular workflow. Stable, but you usually run them only when something specific is needed (a regression, a release bump, an investigation).

| # | Command | What it is | How to use | Reason for using |
|---|---------|------------|------------|------------------|
| 1 | `harness explain` | Shows which composition layer (base / stack / integration / governance) contributed each section of an emitted artifact. | `harness explain <kind>:<name>` where kind ∈ `skill \| agent \| rule`.<br>Examples: `harness explain skill:plan-implementation`, `harness explain agent:impl-planning-agent`, `harness explain rule:surgical-changes`. | When an emitted skill looks wrong, you need to know whether base, a stack overlay, or a governance profile wrote that section. Layer-trace answers it. |
| 2 | `harness list` | Inventory of what the templates know about or what TODO slots remain. | Subcommands:<br>• `harness list templates` — stacks / integrations / governance profiles available.<br>• `harness list todos` — unfilled `*-patterns` slot count. Flag: `--out / -o <dir>` (where `.claude/` lives). | Discovery: "what overlays are available?" and "what's still scaffold-only and needs filling?" |
| 3 | `harness audit` | Governance check on the emitted output — verifies the active governance profile's rules are honoured. | `harness audit`.<br>Flags: `--out / -o <dir>` (where `.claude/` lives), `--strict` (unfilled TODO slots fail). | The compliance-overlay gate. Catches missing PII / retention / encryption clauses in a HIPAA / PCI / SOX / GDPR profile before they ship. Run only on projects under a strict governance profile. |
| 4 | `harness tune` | Friction report aggregated across recent sessions — per-skill failure rates, per-phase intervention counts. | `harness tune --last 10`.<br>Flags: `--project / -p <slug>`, `--last / -n <count>` (default 5), `--out / -o <path>` (default `docs/tuning/tuning-<TS>.md`), `--threshold-interventions <float>` (default 2.0), `--threshold-intervention-rate <0-1>` (default 0.5), `--threshold-blocked <0-1>` (default 0.2). | The data behind "which skill keeps failing the same way?" Drives priority for skill body fixes. |
| 5 | `harness carry-forward` | Manage `pending_carry_forward[]` items across features — list, show details, resolve with evidence, defer with a target date. Reads/writes the per-feature `docs/features/<id>/state.json` files. | Subcommands:<br>• `harness carry-forward list` — flag: `--feature / -f <id>` to filter to one.<br>• `harness carry-forward show <item-id>`<br>• `harness carry-forward resolve <item-id> --evidence <path>` (evidence required)<br>• `harness carry-forward defer <item-id> --until <YYYY-MM-DD>` (date required) | The CLI surface for the carry-forward gate. Lets you triage deferred TCs / risks / findings outside Claude Code. |
| 6 | `harness upgrade` | Bumps `template_version` in `harness.yaml`, re-renders, and shows a diff with deprecation warnings. | `harness upgrade --to <version>` (required).<br>Flag: `--dry-run` (show diff without writing). | Safe template migrations. The diff lets you see what new lint rules / state fields / step files arrive before you commit the upgrade. |
| 7 | `harness fill` | Auto-fills TODO slots in scaffold-only `*-patterns` skill bodies via the local `claude` CLI. | `harness fill <skill-name>` for one, or `harness fill --all` for every `*-patterns` skill with TODOs.<br>Flags: `--all`, `--yes / -y` (skip confirm prompt), `--dry-run` (show diff, never write), `--project-dir <path>` (default `.claude`), `--config / -c <path>`. Requires `claude` CLI installed and authenticated. | Stack overlays ship as scaffolds with TODO slots; this lets you have Claude itself draft the patterns content rather than hand-authoring. Review the output before commit. |
| 8 | `harness plugin` | Pack the harness as a Claude Code plugin or emit a marketplace.json entry. | Subcommands:<br>• `harness plugin pack` — flag: `--out / -o <dir>` (default `dist`).<br>• `harness plugin marketplace` — flag: `--out <path>` (default `marketplace.json`).<br>_(Both deferred to v2; currently stubs.)_ | Share a configured harness across teams or list it on a Claude Code plugin marketplace. |

---

## Slash commands

Commands you invoke inside a Claude Code session. Ordered by typical SDLC flow — setup once, then requirement → implementation → validation → maintenance. Different surface from the `harness` CLI [above](#cli-commands).

| # | Command | What it is | How to use | Reason for using |
|---|---------|------------|------------|------------------|
| 1 | `/arh-init` | First-run harness initializer — walks MCP discovery, populates `CLAUDE.md`, tracker configs, `project-commands.yaml`, RTM stub, ADR-0001. | `/arh-init` (no args) | Sets up the wiring every downstream command depends on. Run once, immediately after the first `harness generate`. |
| 2 | `/arh-scaffold` | Greenfield code skeleton — invokes scaffold-agent with `<framework>-patterns` skills per declared stack. | `/arh-scaffold` (no args) | Use on greenfield only. Skip on brownfield — `/import` (see helpful commands) is the brownfield equivalent. |
| 3 | `/arh-create-prd` | Product-level PRD creation via structured Q&A — 5 interview rounds, domain detection (healthcare / fintech / defense), persistent drafts, and resume support. | `/arh-create-prd` for a new PRD; `/arh-create-prd --resume {slug}` to continue an in-progress session; `/arh-create-prd <reference-file>` to calibrate from an existing document. Produces `docs/prd/{slug}.md`. | Start here when work begins at the product-vision or business-requirement level, before any story intake. Pass the output to `/arh-intake docs/prd/{slug}.md` when ready to generate stories. |
| 4 | `/arh-generate-prd` | Reference-material-driven PRD generation — reads files from a folder, extracts structured data, runs a gap analysis, asks targeted clarification questions (max 3 rounds, ≤4 per round), then writes a complete 15-section PRD. Unresolved gaps become `{TBD}`. | `/arh-generate-prd <reference-folder-path>` for a new session (default: `docs/reference-material`); `/arh-generate-prd --resume {slug}` to continue an interrupted session. The folder must contain a `reference-manifest.json` that maps files to categories and marks which are required — auto-generated if missing. Produces `docs/prd/{slug}.md`. | Use when you already have reference material (specs, research docs, meeting notes, compliance guides). Reads first, asks only what's genuinely missing. Prefer `/arh-create-prd` when starting from scratch. Pass the output to `/arh-intake docs/prd/{slug}.md` when ready. |
| 5 | `/arh-intake` | Parses a requirement source into validated stories, syncs to tracker. Auto-configures integrations on first run. | `/arh-intake <file \| raw text \| tracker URL \| doc URL>` | The canonical entry point. Always run before `/arh-research` or `/plan-*` — it generates the story id everything else hangs off. |
| 6 | `/arh-validate-story` | Scores a story against a 6-dimension rubric (clarity, testability, scope, NFRs, edge cases, traceability). Pass ≥80/100, no dimension under 60. | `/arh-validate-story <story-id>` | Catches story-level gaps before research wastes time on a bad spec. Self-corrects up to 3 rounds, then escalates. |
| 7 | `/arh-plan-program` | Full release planning orchestrator — 8-step pipeline: change classification, dependency inference with optional research enrichment, structural + adversarial validation, blocking human sign-off at Step 5, deterministic wave planning, interactive dependency graph, and Gantt chart. Locks the dependency topology once per release. | `/arh-plan-program` (no args) | Runs once per release across the full validated story set. All stories must be `Validated` or beyond. Step 5 (Human Review Gate) requires explicit sign-off before the pipeline proceeds to wave planning. Topology is immutable after sign-off; use `/arh-refresh-graph` for status badge updates without re-planning. |
| 8 | `/arh-refresh-graph` | Refresh the interactive dependency graph with current story statuses and research badges without re-inferring topology. Safe to run any time. | `/arh-refresh-graph` (no args) | Use whenever story statuses change (Draft → In Progress → Done) or after `/arh-research` adds research reports. Keeps the visual dependency graph current without re-running the full `/arh-plan-program` pipeline. The dependency topology (edges, waves, critical path) stays locked. |
| 9 | `/arh-research` | Codebase feasibility check — exploration log, pattern map, risk register, GO / GO-WITH-CONDITIONS / SPIKE / BLOCK verdict. | `/arh-research <story-id>` | Tells you whether to plan or to spike. Skipping this is how teams build features the codebase can't actually support. |
| 10 | `/plan-requirements` | Story + research → `REQUIREMENTS.md` + structured test cases. Includes a Product Gate (APPROVE / CHANGES). | `/plan-requirements <story-id>` | The PO sign-off boundary. Without an APPROVE here, `/plan-implementation` runs against unsettled requirements. |
| 11 | `/plan-implementation` | `REQUIREMENTS.md` → `PLAN.md` with mini-ADRs, file plan, module hierarchy, task breakdown, test strategy. | `/plan-implementation <story-id>` | Turns "what" into "how." Incomplete plan in → incomplete code out; the plan-validation rubric enforces 5 completeness dimensions. |
| 12 | `/implement` | Branch → code → local checks → fix loop → commit-PR gates → tracker comment. Strict surgical-changes rule. | `/implement <story-id>` | The actual building. Per-task state persistence enables `--resume`; multiple gates (carry-forward, agent-flags) hold the line at commit time. |
| 13 | `/clarify` | Bundles mid-stream `[NEEDS CLARIFICATION]` markers across a feature's docs into ONE PO-facing question round per session. | `/clarify <story-id>` to bundle and post; `/clarify <story-id> --apply` to fold answers back into the source docs | Stops the anti-pattern of pinging the PO 9 times per session. One round, one tracker comment, one paper trail. |
| 14 | `/human-review` | Triages every agent-raised flag (sensitive defaults, copy-paste shapes, risky patterns, dead code, inconsistencies) with accept / reject / defer verdicts. | `/human-review <story-id>` | The agent already notices these — this stops them getting buried in chat. Commit-PR is gated on zero open flags. |
| 15 | `/validate-feature` | Generates or reruns E2E flows from test-case JSON, runs them against real APIs, emits a consolidated bug-style report. | `/validate-feature <story-id>` | The "does it actually work" gate. Unit tests in `/implement` aren't enough; this catches integration bugs before `/review` sees green. |
| 16 | `/review` | Six-dimension architectural code review — coupling, layering, error handling, edge cases, naming, doc / runbook coverage. Severity-ranked findings. | `/review <story-id \| pr-url \| pr-number \| branch>` or empty for current diff | Catches what was correct-to-spec but wrong-shape. Separate from `/security-review`'s scope. |
| 17 | `/security-review` | Multi-phase security gate — SAST grep, dep scan, OWASP checklist, stack-pattern check, compliance overlay. Blocks Critical / High. | `/security-review <story-id>` | Final gate before merge. Compliance-tagged carry-forwards trigger unconditional block here, regardless of `--accept-pending`. |

**Note on `/decide`.** Not a user-invokable command — it's a *knowledge skill* loaded automatically by the impl-planning-agent during `/plan-implementation` Phase 1. It writes a structured entry to `state[<id>].decisions[]` alongside each mini-ADR in `PLAN.md §1`. You don't type `/decide`; you see its output as the queryable decision record on the feature's site dashboard.

**Note on `root-cause-first`.** Also a *knowledge skill*, not a command. It codifies the debugging discipline — state the root cause before any fix, patch the cause not the symptom, and after 3 failed fixes question the architecture instead of attempting a 4th. Loaded by `implementation-agent` (so `/implement`'s fix-loop and inline fixes obey it) and by `/fix`. You never type it; it shapes how every defect gets fixed.

### Helpful commands

> _Experimental commands._ These ship in the harness today and work, but their behaviour, arguments, and write surfaces may change between minor versions. Treat as opt-in extras, not load-bearing parts of the core SDLC flow above.

| # | Command | What it is | How to use | Reason for using |
|---|---------|------------|------------|------------------|
| 1 | `/setup-ci` | Generates CI pipeline config from stack-overlay recipes for the configured CI provider. | `/setup-ci` (no args) | Detects existing workflow conflicts. Run after the stack is declared via `harness add stack`. |
| 2 | `/explain` | Show full lineage for a feature — story, research, PRD, plan, impl branch, validation, review, security, decisions, clarifications, flags. | `/explain <story-id>` | The "what's going on with this feature" view. Pulls directly from state, so it's always current. |
| 3 | `/import` | Brownfield bulk backfill — pulls existing Jira/Linear stories, Confluence/Notion pages, local files, and code into harness state. Idempotent. | `/import` (no args) | Onboarding a repo with PRs already in flight. Without this, Harness starts from zero; this gives it the history. |
| 4 | `/sync` | Pull-on-demand bidirectional tracker sync — three-way merge with manual edits. | `/sync` for all in-flight, or `/sync <story-id>` for one | The world outside the repo changes — tracker statuses, doc edits, comments from the PO. Without `/sync`, harness state drifts. |
| 5 | `/trace` | Refresh / verify the Requirements Traceability Matrix. `--verify` re-derives source SHAs and flags stale rows. | `/trace [--verify]` | Catches RTM drift before `/review` reads stale lineage. |
| 6 | `/fix` | Hotfix lane — fix one defect without the full SDLC (no story/arh-research/PRD/plan). Root-cause-first, surgical, regression-tested, evidence-gated; bounces architectural defects back to `/arh-intake`. `--debug` = investigate-only (writes an RCA report, no code change). | `/fix "<bug>"` · `/fix --from-test <path>` · `/fix <TRACKER-KEY>` · add `--for <id>` to attach to a feature · add `--debug` to diagnose only | The lane for a one-line bug or production hotfix that shouldn't need a story + plan. The architectural-bounce keeps it from being a governance backdoor — anything needing an ADR routes to the full flow. |

---

## State data structure (two-tier, schema v3)

Each feature's SDLC progress is the source of truth for downstream gates. State splits across two locations:

- **`docs/state/features.json`** — the **INDEX**. A JSON object keyed by feature id (`<EPIC>-<SEQ>`, e.g. `BKM-03`), one status-mirror entry per feature. Cross-feature readers (SessionStart hook, `harness site` dashboards, `/trace`) read this single small file. Pre-plan phases (`/arh-intake`, `/arh-validate-story`, `/arh-research`, `/import`) write the full record here.
- **`docs/features/<id>/state.json`** — the **per-feature FULL RECORD**. Created at `/plan-requirements` Phase 1 (the migration point). Holds the heavy audit arrays. Post-plan phases write here as primary and mirror the small status fields back to the index.

Each phase hardcodes which file it reads/writes — no runtime fallback. Status fields hold short literals only — never tracker keys, branch names, or freeform strings. Tracker ids live in dedicated `tracker_*` fields.

Full schema with the field-ownership table (tier legend: **I**=index-only, **P**=per-feature-only, **B**=both/mirrored) is written to `docs/state/SCHEMA.md` at `harness generate` time and described in [documents/state-contract.md](documents/state-contract.md). The tables below are the at-a-glance reference; pre-plan fields live in the index, audit arrays are all **P**-tier (per-feature file only).

### Phase-status fields (status literals only)

These drive the phase-preconditions gate order. Downstream phases refuse to start unless the prior phase's literal is set to its terminal value.

| Field | Allowed literals | Set by |
|---|---|---|
| `story` | `draft`, `validated`, `escalated`, `imported:<source>` | `/arh-intake`, `/arh-validate-story`, `/import` |
| `story_priority` | `P1`, `P2`, `P3` | `/arh-intake` |
| `research` | `pending`, `complete`, `skipped:<reason>` | `/arh-research` |
| `research_verdict` | `GO`, `GO-WITH-CONDITIONS`, `SPIKE`, `BLOCK` | `/arh-research` |
| `prd` | `pending`, `complete` | `/plan-requirements` |
| `gate` | `PENDING`, `APPROVE`, `CHANGES` | `/plan-requirements` Phase 4 (product gate) |
| `plan` | `pending`, `complete` | `/plan-implementation` Phase 5 |
| `plan_validation` | `PASS`, `FAIL`, `ESCALATED` | `/plan-implementation` Phase 3b |
| `plan_validation_rounds` | `1` or `2` | `/plan-implementation` Phase 3b |
| `impl` | `pending`, `complete` | `/implement` Step 5 |
| `validation` | `pending`, `passed`, `failed` | `/validate-feature` |
| `review` | `PASS`, `PASS WITH WARNINGS`, `BLOCKED` | `/review` |
| `security` | `pending`, `PASS`, `BLOCKED` | `/security-review` |
| `phase` | `imported` / `story` / `story-validated` / `research` / `plan-requirements` / `plan-requirements-approved` / `plan-implementation` / `implementation` / `review` / `security-reviewed` | every phase's state-write step |

### Audit arrays (the structured trails)

Each array accumulates per-event records over the feature's lifetime. The site generator (`harness site`) surfaces every one of these as a per-feature page.

| Field | Item shape (key fields) | Written by |
|---|---|---|
| `impl_tasks[]` | `task_id`, `status` (`done` \| `blocked` \| `skipped`), `completed_at`, `files_touched`, `reason` | `/implement` Step 1 (per task — enables `--resume`) |
| `impl_evidence.checks{}` | six-dimension handover packet — `typecheck`, `unit_tests`, `lint`, `runtime`, `compile`, `design_check`, each with `status` (`PASS` \| `FAIL` \| `N/A`), `command`, `exit_code`, `evidence_path`, `flag_id`, `ran_at` | `evidence-pass` skill at end of `/implement` Step 1 |
| `decisions[]` | `decision_id`, `title`, `alternatives`, `chosen`, `rationale`, `blast_radius`, `reversibility`, `adr_ref` | `decide` skill (in `/plan-implementation` Phase 1) |
| `clarifications[]` | per round: `round`, `asked_at`, `questions[]` (with `qid`, `marker`, `source_doc`, `category`, `answer`, `applied_at`), `tracker_comment`, `status` | `/clarify` Phase 2 (bundle) + Phase 4 (apply) |
| `agent_flags[]` | `flag_id` (`AF-NN`), `kind`, `summary`, `source`, `status` (`open` \| `accept` \| `reject` \| `defer`), `decision`, `decided_by`, `decided_at`, `rationale`, `carry_forward_ref` | `/human-review` Phase 2 (the only writer) |
| `pending_carry_forward[]` | `item_id`, `kind` (`test_case` \| `task` \| `risk` \| `finding`), `reason`, `owner`, `added_by`, `resolved_at`, `evidence` | `/plan-implementation`, `/validate-feature`, `/review`, `/human-review` (deferred items) |
| `adrs_referenced[]` | list of ADR ids cited in PLAN.md `## Architecture Decisions` | `/implement` Step 5 |
| `security_findings{}` | severity counts: `critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low`, plus `tool_missing[]` | `/security-review` |

### Tracker keys, paths, and housekeeping

These are scalar metadata fields — separate from the status literals on purpose, so that gates never confuse a tracker key (`BM-4`) with a status (`validated`).

| Field | Shape | What it carries | Written by |
|---|---|---|---|
| `impl_branch` | string | feature branch name (e.g. `feature/BKM-03`) | `/implement` Step 5 |
| `validation_summary` | string | `<DATE> P=<P>/<TOTAL> in <N> round(s)` one-line summary | `/validate-feature` |
| `review_report` | path | `docs/features/<id>/REVIEW.md` | `/review` |
| `security_report` | path | `docs/features/<id>/SECURITY-<DATE>.md` | `/security-review` |
| `tracker_story` / `tracker_research` / `tracker_prd` / `tracker_plan` / `tracker_review_comment` | tracker key | external ids (Jira / Linear / Confluence) | `issue-tracking-agent` (per phase) |
| `governance_profile` | literal | `standard`, `strict`, `hipaa`, `pci`, `sox`, `gdpr` | composer at `harness generate` |
| `rtm_source_sha` | git sha | source ref captured when the RTM row was created | `/arh-intake`, `/import`, `/trace` |
| `sync_baseline{}` | object | per-field remote snapshots used by `/sync`'s three-way merge | `/sync` |
| `last_synced_at` | iso8601 | timestamp of last successful `/sync` | `/sync` |
| `needs_clarification_count` | int | count of open `[NEEDS CLARIFICATION]` markers | `/arh-intake`, `/plan-requirements` |
| `last_updated` | iso8601 | timestamp of last write to this record | every phase |

### Example (excerpt — `BKM-03` after `/implement` finishes)

```jsonc
{
  "BKM-03": {
    "story": "validated",
    "story_priority": "P1",
    "research": "complete", "research_verdict": "GO-WITH-CONDITIONS",
    "prd": "complete", "gate": "APPROVE",
    "plan": "complete", "plan_validation": "PASS",
    "impl": "complete", "impl_branch": "feature/BKM-03",
    "impl_tasks": [ /* T-01 … T-23, each with status: done */ ],
    "impl_evidence": {
      "session_ended_at": "2026-05-22T17:10:00Z",
      "rounds": 1,
      "checks": {
        "typecheck": {"status": "PASS", "exit_code": 0, "evidence_path": "docs/features/BKM-03/evidence/typecheck.log", "flag_id": null, "...": "..."},
        "design_check": {"status": "N/A", "flag_id": "AF-05", "...": "..."}
        /* + unit_tests, lint, runtime, compile */
      }
    },
    "validation": "passed", "review": "PASS",
    "agent_flags": [ /* populated by /human-review after triage */ ],
    "pending_carry_forward": [ {"item_id": "R-07-offset-pagination", "kind": "risk", "...": "..." } ],
    "phase": "review",
    "tracker_story": "BM-4", "tracker_plan": "BM-25",
    "last_updated": "2026-05-22T16:35:00Z"
  }
}
```

## Contributing

> **Start here:** [ARCHITECTURE.md](ARCHITECTURE.md) — code map, invariants, and extension recipes (add a lint rule, a stack scaffold, an integration, an emitter target, a CLI command, a state field).

### Setup

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Apexon-Enterprise-org/agent-rise-foundry.git
cd harness
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e ".[dev]"
pytest -q                        # 307 tests, must stay green
ruff check src/                  # lint
```

### Commit convention

Conventional Commits drives auto-release. Use one of these prefixes:

| Prefix | Effect on next release |
|---|---|
| `feat:` / `feat(scope):` | **minor** bump |
| `fix:` / `perf:` | **patch** bump |
| `feat!:` or commit body containing `BREAKING CHANGE` | **major** bump (once out of 0.x) |
| `docs:` / `chore:` / `test:` / `ci:` / `style:` / `refactor:` / `build:` | no release |

Examples from recent history:

```
feat(site): harness site — MkDocs site generator for non-technical readers
fix(site): file:// browsing + sidebar scrolling for many features
docs(readme): add quick-start section
ci: auto-release pipeline via GitHub Actions (tag-less, pyproject version)
```

### Pull request workflow

1. Branch from `main`: `git checkout -b feat/<short-slug>` or `fix/<short-slug>`.
2. Make changes; add or update tests in `tests/unit/`.
3. Run `pytest -q` and `ruff check src/` locally.
4. Open the PR. GitHub Actions runs `ruff` + `pytest` + smoke wheel build automatically.
5. On merge to `main`, the pipeline auto-bumps the version, tags, and (when configured) publishes to PyPI. No manual version edits.

Releases, version bump rules, and pipeline secrets are detailed in [documents/releases.md](documents/releases.md).

### Architecture invariants

Before substantial changes, read [CLAUDE.md](CLAUDE.md) — covers the surface-separation rule (command → agent → skill → rule → hook), state-contract literals, the scaffold-only policy for stack/integration/governance overlays, and the phase-preconditions gate order.

